    "body": "Fixes #8103.\n\nIterating over a literal sequence of one-character bytes literals inferred a C character item type, so the loop variable received integers instead of bytes objects:\n\n```python\nfor c in [b'a', b'b']:\n    ...\n# c == 97, 98 instead of b'a', b'b'\n```\n\nSingle-character `str` literals still iterate over `Py_UCS4`, which converts back to `str`.\n\nThis is split out of #8114 as suggested in review, to allow an independent decision about what change to apply to which legacy branches. The mixed item type part (e.g. `(123, 123.45)` or `[True, 2]`) remains in #8114.\n\nNew and updated tests are in `tests/run/type_inference.pyx` and `tests/run/for_in_iter.py`.\n",
    "closed_at": null,
